course: dictionary
lesson: 2924
title: Mild words for annoyance
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «"Drat", "blast", "sugar", "fudge" and "darn" are mild stand-ins for swear words.» A stand-in is a word used instead of another word. Drat is a soft, old-fashioned way to show you are annoyed, without using a rude word.
explain: Drat is an interjection, a short word that bursts out on its own to show a feeling, like ouch or oh no. You can say it alone, or as «drat it» or «drat that dog» when something has gone wrong.
explain: Use the clues around the word. When someone wants to say something rude but chooses a gentler word, they are using a stand-in. So drat must mean a mild, old-fashioned "oh, how annoying!" It is not about being happy or surprised.
fact: Drat is an interjection: a short word that shows a feeling, such as annoyance.
fact: «Drat» means a mild exclamation of annoyance.
fact: Drat is a soft stand-in for «damn», so people use it when they want to sound annoyed but not rude.
fact: Drat sounds old-fashioned; you might hear it in cartoons, old books, or from grandparents.
fact: Drat is safe for children to use, and it is understood in every region.
exercise: «"Drat", "blast", "sugar", "fudge" and "darn" are mild stand-ins for swear words.» What are drat and blast in this sentence? | Animals that live in the sea | *Mild stand-ins for swear words | Old names for towns | Words for very big numbers
exercise: Grandad says, «Drat that dog!» What does drat show? | *Mild annoyance | Great happiness | Sleepiness | A question about the time
exercise: Which word from the sentence is another mild stand-in, like drat? | castle | swear | sugar bowl | *darn
test: Drat is a word that bursts out to show a feeling. What kind of word is it? | Adjective | Verb | Noun | *Interjection
test: Why might someone say «drat» instead of a rude word? | To ask a question | To sound happy and thankful | *To sound annoyed without being rude | To tell a secret
exam: Grandad says «Drat it» when he drops his teacup. What does he mean? | He wants another cup of tea | He is fast asleep | Hooray, he is pleased | *He is mildly annoyed
